{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $V(2,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $V$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & =\\frac{8(2)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-71213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y-2)^{2}-{x}^{2}=4"] ["math" "4x=\\pm (y-2)"] ["math" "y-2=4{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(2,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2-{0}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{5}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5 & =x-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the parabola $x={y}^{2}+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=1-2{y}^{2}"] ["math" "x=3-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "x=3{y}^{2}+1"] ["math" "x={y}^{2}+2"] ["math" "x={y}^{2}+5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={y}^{2}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $R(3,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,2) & =20-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+{y}^{2}=20"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-{y}^{2}=20"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=23"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=23"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =1(1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-2 & =-1 \\\\ xy & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the hyperbola $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2x+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{x}-2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{x}"] ["math" "y=x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-128447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $Q(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $Q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y-1)^{2}-{x}^{2}=1"] ["math" "x=\\pm (y-1)"] ["math" "y-1={x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-36825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=0$ gives a parabola\n- $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {y}^{2} & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-1){y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $x=0,$ which is the $y$-axis. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$  then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, the only real solution to this equation is  $(x,y)=(0,0),$ which is not in the domain of $f(x,y)$ because it would result in division by zero. Thus, III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the listed"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=3$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=1$ gives a circle" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4 & =c{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(3-c){y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ which is a circle.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives an ellipse\n- $c=2$ gives two lines\n- $c=3$ gives a hyperbola" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{y}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ c{x}^{2}+(c-2){y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71401" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin\n- $c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a line\n- $c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin\n- $c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} & =c \\\\ x+{y}^{2} & =cx \\\\ x(1-c)+{y}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curves is $x=-{y}^{2},$ which is a parabola that opens to the left (excluding the origin). So, II is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c>0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ {e}^{xy} & =c \\\\ xy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c>0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡c}{x}.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c=0.$ In this case, we have the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\operatorname{ln}⁡c<0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y=-\\frac{|\\operatorname{ln}⁡c|}{x}.$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/d330ddb12d1df9d31af3d082b04271d02df381739064a6fe2203dabce358a09f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/24b6123a1f641df32cdc19ec77d999dac5584b9693ae92ce9c1e618f67487473.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/57f87a716b14698d0087bc65e9abdc04776bd4b7addaaf172a4e5f0a5f38ea6a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/836e10a411261b1ef2cb8146aed75c40326f278487e5843c57f10f65b9cc3f05.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/d330ddb12d1df9d31af3d082b04271d02df381739064a6fe2203dabce358a09f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/ef3171d89bff6a2a6ef105f81a9fe60959f2d760634039cd8fb0f70cf8c2cc9e.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/d330ddb12d1df9d31af3d082b04271d02df381739064a6fe2203dabce358a09f.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of ellipses centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cccaa6d7f554076b3a79364e1f2c4c061ae7cd86f7f0064e3861ba3b035b6a3e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/1b1cd5983d4a19990cc5e82726bb8cd8101dbab0f2dc7c9934a64c45e6126ee4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/7ab95ed367277627d5baf535db74994d7d02cd41deae939d76ed08e22799ff40.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/7c4530dc74747fc0bdae0ca4c8608a72dbf03ca413d4479c8a4b9224246ac01f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cccaa6d7f554076b3a79364e1f2c4c061ae7cd86f7f0064e3861ba3b035b6a3e.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-71409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cccaa6d7f554076b3a79364e1f2c4c061ae7cd86f7f0064e3861ba3b035b6a3e.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-2{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =2{x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/4c259d100b5e63a37822df6f7a5b5a6493efdb13b147a76a0183d120e6aeec5b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/56e4941d265e1fc778e7b5672a14f38d45ba43b13562f3ddab53cebd5175cea4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6f16e785aaef98bf6609a4ef79b6574e3650110607680da5fdbb8034eb590457.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/79d8db55b0de544c328188d2af444d848658b65626e753bdc1654791cc457506.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-64908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/6521da99a82e38a68a5aa87a0c7c88eacf4ed7b78351af594ec309fa2cb7f57b.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-x) & =c \\\\ y-x & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =x+{e}^{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of lines with slope of $1.$ Also, note that ${e}^{c}>0$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/0bedc560b7b18c2a6841313d888dde7349b66f2514ce8c222af7ef2fc0bc9546.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/5155660bc0e9957b1a33cc6fbd933ba10618be07911f9384255bc703f7925ba7.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/93cd9ed4c9c1507d1ff828abbb697cd72ab8ebdb7d44a1033d6e99a1eed9e633.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/d5e28121cd5847f3119a9fb33a57dd2d86e2f12bff3061ef64182b12c1ace178.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129116" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-3,3].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-3,3].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =c \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\frac{c}{3}+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $y.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-3,3]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of vertical lines, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "907f8183ae59986c36626dc380b93a2844fad52c1cefdc3bce70fd3eea1de6c5"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/2c570cc9958db69a30dc400920b6ff27574e79bd26a53b35e086cb03034c5b6d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/d774c444ad33c68ec5d507611324030f2005671265ba5f855f322d8db57b69b4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/ef27f12eb1ee6872210bf8cadb6908efd31c1bb54e9e7d34cc3456df2ee03a77.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "q-129113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/cdd8cec2d30ff2956d5210a49036c17d99781d0b62c0b0f42f97d0575c4d80f6.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac79b35864ea9dcca5746cf98f5f8a963ef7098632b1972877b41fa813692a78"} {"question" "e-5968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-5968.json" "file_sha256" "4a141dc76b2e8f4e22cf084d4d8f85c1c2994c747c37bc43c76e6fae644e4f30"} {"question" "e-5968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+4y+4) & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola $x^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-5968.json" "file_sha256" "4a141dc76b2e8f4e22cf084d4d8f85c1c2994c747c37bc43c76e6fae644e4f30"} {"question" "e-4228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives a circle\n\n- $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\n- $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-4228.json" "file_sha256" "ad5611ea8f1541caab5895c1a508eb4fdc17e9cae7aef2edda01fa1c8305f8d8"} {"question" "e-4228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-4228.json" "file_sha256" "ad5611ea8f1541caab5895c1a508eb4fdc17e9cae7aef2edda01fa1c8305f8d8"} {"question" "e-4229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-4229.json" "file_sha256" "9ec36863aa3f733878338a949e4b2abc9bcc60df1010594b5b4d5a5ccf6c7cb3"} {"question" "e-4229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}$ take the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c.\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0.$ In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c.$\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/assets/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042279/example-4229.json" "file_sha256" "9ec36863aa3f733878338a949e4b2abc9bcc60df1010594b5b4d5a5ccf6c7cb3"}] "usage" {"e-5968" [] "e-4229" [] "q-64908" [] "e-4228" []} "basis" 1544 "retractions" [[17592186125095 :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x, y) = \\ln (y - 2x^{2})$?"] [17592186124280 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}$."] [17592186124280 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= c \\\\\n\\frac{12y}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= 3 \\\\\n4y &= x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - y^{2} - 4y - 4 &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y^{2} + 4y + 4) &= -4 \\\\\nx^{2} - (y + 2)^{2} &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola\n\n$$\nx^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4\n$$"] [17592186124202 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c=1$ gives a circle\n2. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n3. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola"] [17592186124202 :question/worked-solution "Setting\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2}{x^{2}} &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 &= cx^{2} \\\\\n(1 - c)x^{2} + 2y^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1$, then the level curve is $y^2 = 1$, which consists of two lines.\n- If $c = 0$, then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2$, which is an ellipse.\n- If $c = 2$, then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."] [17592186124203 :question/problem "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}$."] [17592186124203 :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}\n$$\n\ntake the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ne^{x^{2} - y^{2}} = c; \\ln (e^{x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\ln c; x^{2} - y^{2} = \\ln c\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c$.\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c$.\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0$. In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x$.\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c$.\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1900/Source/Images/d07a616a086987c45b478dda907c99c1.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123593 17592186123594 17592186123595 17592186123596 17592186123597] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123592 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123592 :answer-field/id] [17592186123592 :answer-field/key] [17592186123592 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
